================================================================================
                         GIT COMMANDS CHEAT SHEET & THEORY GUIDE
================================================================================

Git manages code changes across three primary zones:
1. Working Directory - Actual files on your machine
2. Staging Area     - Draft changes prepared for saving
3. Repository       - Permanent snapshot history (local and remote)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. git init
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Initializes a brand new, empty local Git repository in your current directory. 
It creates a hidden .git directory containing all the tracking metadata, 
objects, and commit history structure Git needs to record changes.

EXAMPLE:
  mkdir my-app
  cd my-app
  git init
  # Output: Initialized empty Git repository in /path/to/my-app/.git/

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. git clone
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Downloads an existing remote repository (from GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, etc.) 
to your local machine. It creates a local directory, copies all project files, 
downloads the entire commit history, and automatically configures a link named 
'origin' pointing back to the remote source.

EXAMPLE:
  git clone https://github.com/user/repository-name.git
  cd repository-name

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. git status
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Inspects the current state of your working directory and staging area. It shows 
which files are untracked (new files Git isn't watching yet), modified (edited 
but not staged), or staged (prepared for the next commit), as well as which 
branch you are currently on.

EXAMPLE:
  git status

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. git add
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Moves changes from your working directory into the Staging Area (also called 
the index). This lets you prepare and select specific changes before permanently 
committing them to history.

EXAMPLE:
  # Stage a single file
  git add index.html

  # Stage all new, modified, and deleted files in the project
  git add .

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. git commit
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Takes a snapshot of all files currently in the staging area and permanently 
saves that snapshot to your local Git repository log. Every commit gets a 
unique SHA-1 hash ID and requires a message describing what was changed.

EXAMPLE:
  git commit -m "Add responsive navigation header"

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. git push
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Uploads your local commits from your local machine to a remote repository 
(like GitHub). It merges your local commit history into the target branch on 
the remote server so collaborators can see your updates.

EXAMPLE:
  # Pushes local 'main' branch commits to the 'origin' remote repository
  git push origin main

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. git pull
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Downloads the latest commits from a remote repository branch and immediately 
merges them into your current local working branch. Under the hood, 'git pull' 
is a combination of two operations: 'git fetch' (downloading data) + 'git merge' 
(incorporating changes).

EXAMPLE:
  # Pulls and merges updates from the remote 'main' branch into your active branch
  git pull origin main

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. git branch
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Manages independent lines of development. Under the hood, a branch in Git is 
simply a lightweight, movable pointer to a specific commit. Creating a branch 
allows you to build features or fix bugs in isolation without disturbing the 
main codebase.

EXAMPLE:
  # List all local branches (current branch marked with *)
  git branch

  # Create a new branch
  git branch feature-login

  # Delete a branch locally (after merging)
  git branch -d feature-login

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. git checkout / git switch
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Navigates between branches or inspects older commits. When you checkout a branch, 
Git updates the files in your working directory to match the snapshot of that 
branch's latest commit and moves the HEAD pointer to it.
(Note: 'git switch' is the modern command dedicated to branch switching).

EXAMPLE:
  # Switch to an existing branch
  git checkout feature-login
  # OR using modern alternative:
  git switch feature-login

  # Shortcut: Create AND switch to a new branch in one step
  git checkout -b feature-login
  # OR:
  git switch -c feature-login

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. git merge
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Combines the commit histories of two different branches into one. You switch 
to your target branch (e.g., main) and merge another branch (e.g., feature-login) 
into it. If both branches edited the same lines of code, Git will pause and ask 
you to resolve a merge conflict.

EXAMPLE:
  # 1. Switch to the main branch
  git switch main

  # 2. Merge feature branch into main
  git merge feature-login

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11. git stash
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Temporarily stores ("stashes") your modified or staged changes without making 
a commit. This gives you a clean working directory so you can switch branches 
quickly to work on urgent fixes, then restore your draft work later.

EXAMPLE:
  # Save local uncommitted work to stash
  git stash

  # Re-apply stashed changes and remove them from stash list
  git stash pop

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
12. git log
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Displays the commit history of the current branch. It lists commits in reverse 
chronological order, showing each commit's unique SHA hash ID, author, date, 
and commit message.

EXAMPLE:
  # Show full commit history
  git log

  # Compact view showing each commit on a single line
  git log --oneline

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
13. git reset
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Undoes local changes or moves the HEAD pointer back to a previous commit in 
history. Depending on the flag used, it can keep your file edits (--soft or 
default) or completely erase all uncommitted edits (--hard).

EXAMPLE:
  # Unstage files added with 'git add', keeping changes in working directory
  git reset

  # Discard last commit but keep file changes in staging area
  git reset --soft HEAD~1

  # Permanently wipe out last commit AND all uncommitted work
  git reset --hard HEAD~1

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14. git restore
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THEORY:
Discards uncommitted changes in your working directory or unstages files from 
the staging area without affecting repository commit history. It is designed as 
a safer alternative to 'git checkout' for reverting files.

EXAMPLE:
  # Discard local changes in a specific file
  git restore index.html

  # Remove a file from the staging area (unstage it)
  git restore --staged index.html
